Mom’s hands smooth the front of my jacket flat, once, twice, tugging the hem down over my shorts until it hangs straight. Then she slides one arm under my knees and lifts me onto her hip, settling me there like a bag she’s carried a hundred times.

“No more wandering,” she says into my hair. “You stay up here with me. I mean it, Anzu.”

I don’t argue. I tuck my elbow against her ribs and my hand goes back flat over the pocket. My cheek ends up on her shoulder, and from there I can see Dad coming across the grass at a hard run, arms pumping, sandals kicking up little clods of dirt. Ichigo is right behind him, and he isn’t pretending about it. He’s running as fast as his legs go.

“ANZU!” Dad skids to a stop so close that Mom’s hair flutters. His eyes go straight to my arm and the pink stripe running up it, and the color leaves his face. “Blood! Masaki, there’s blood! Is she cut? How deep is it? Where else is she hurt?” He’s already on his knees, hands hovering over me like he’s afraid to land.

“Isshin, breathe,” Mom says.

“I am breathing! I’m a doctor, I breathe professionally!” But his hands are shaking a little when he takes my wrist in both of them. He turns it over and goes slow, thumb tracing along the marks. Then he stops. He tilts my arm toward the light, and the two thin lines resolve into what they are: scratches, parallel, with a bit of green leaf stuck in one. His shoulders drop an inch.

“Prickles,” he says, and lets out a long breath that ruffles my bangs. “Bush prickles. You were playing in the bushes.” A weak laugh escapes him. “Oh, thank goodness. I thought... never mind what I thought.” His fingers press along my elbow, each knuckle, my shoulder, then he tips my chin side to side. “Nothing deep. Nothing broken. Does anything else hurt? Your head? Your legs?”

I shake my head.

“Good girl.” He kisses my knuckles, loudly, and holds my hand up to the light again. “Dirt in all of it, though. Masaki, the water?”

“Already out.” Mom digs in her bag with her free hand and comes up with the bottle and a packet of wipes. “And you, young lady, are not getting off easy. Do you know how scared I was? I called and called. You don’t go off by yourself, not ever, not even to the bushes.”

Dad takes my arm and holds it steady, palm up. Mom tears the packet with her teeth.

The first wipe is cold. Then it isn’t cold at all. It stings so sharply that I squeak, a small, high sound I didn’t decide to make, and my whole arm jerks inside Dad’s grip. My shoulder rises toward my ear. My face does nothing. I stare at the wipe in Mom’s fingers, at the brown and pink smeared across it, and my eyes stay on it, wide and unblinking, and my breath goes short and shallow through my nose.

“I know, I know,” Mom murmurs, and her voice gentles as she slows down. “Almost done. That’s what you get for hiding in prickly things. Next time you ask first, okay?”

“Does it hurt a lot?” Ichigo has pushed in close beside Dad, and he’s bent down to look at my face, his eyebrows pulled together. “Anzu. You can say if it hurts. It’s okay.” He glances up at Mom. “Can I hold her other hand?”

“Go ahead,” Mom says.

He takes my free hand, the one that isn’t on my pocket, and squeezes it in both of his. His palms are warm and a little sticky. I let him.

“Brave! Isn’t that right?” Dad says, his grin wobbling back into place. His eyes slide away for the length of a blink, over my shoulder toward the far side of the field, to the old plane tree. They sit there flat and quiet. Then he turns back to my hand, and the grin is wide again. “A true Kurosaki! The doctor is very proud!”

“Isshin, your voice,” Mom says, still dabbing.

“Her ears are fine, she’s a patient, I’m allowed to—”

“Daaad.” Karin has hold of his belt and is hanging off it, dragging her sneakers through the dirt. “Can we go? I’m hungry. You said snacks.” She squints at my arm for a second, then at my face. “Did you fall in or something?”

“Anzu’s bleeding,” Yuzu says. She’s standing behind Karin with both hands pressed to her mouth, and her eyes have already gone shiny. “Is she gonna be okay? Mama, is Anzu gonna be okay?”

“She’s fine, sweetheart, it’s just scratches.” Mom turns my arm toward her, tapping a last wipe against my wrist. “See? All clean. And she’s going to sit very still at home and think about what she did.”

Dad pinches something out from under my thumbnail with two fingers before I can pull the hand away. “Splinter,” he says, and drops it into the dirt. “There. Now we’re done.”

Ichigo is still holding my hand. “Let’s go home,” he says. “I’ll carry your jacket sleeve so it doesn’t rub.” He tugs the cuff down carefully over the bandage-less scratches and keeps his fingers around it.

“Home it is!” Dad scoops Yuzu up in one arm without even asking her, and she squeals, then hiccups. Karin climbs onto his back, hooking her arms around his neck and her knees under his elbows. He staggers and groans like they weigh a hundred kilos. “Ow! My spine! My poor old spine!”

“You’re not even old,” Karin says, flatly, right by his ear.

We set off down the path. Mom’s hip rocks under me with every step, and the sun is low enough now to hit my eyes whenever I lift my head. Ichigo walks close on her other side, looking up at me every few steps as if to check I’m still there. Dad starts whistling something off-key. Behind us, at the far end of the park, the plane tree sticks up over the grass with its pale strip of bark still hanging down.

I keep my hand where it is.

